{
  "id": "Compound::DB00542 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Benazepril, an Ace Inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hypertension through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Benazepril may act as a causal agent in the development of hypertension, either directly or through intermediaries such as Quinapril. Both Benazepril and Quinapril share a common ancestor in the Ace Inhibitor class, indicating a shared pharmacological mechanism. This class of drugs inhibits the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), leading to vasodilation and reduced blood pressure. The observed relationships suggest that Benazepril may contribute to hypertension through RAAS inhibition, potentially via a direct or indirect pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Ace Inhibitors in modulating blood pressure, with Benazepril possibly exerting a therapeutic effect in hypertension through this mechanism. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Benazepril and hypertension, particularly through its class-specific action on the RAAS.",
